{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:inflation:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:0d850bdbd14b3f38f28bfdcf257cb50118ae51b127c09822227b2a6af011ca71","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":0.9},{"probability":0.5,"value":1.75},{"probability":0.9,"value":2.75}]},"questionHash":"sha256:e1b53adbdbe1bb1ccef339035b6376b49668b9341050c684f480d5b347e316f6","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq3","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"WEIGHTING: Fermi heaviest (his is the only full growth-accounting ledger with two independent cross-checks and an explicit PPP-weight accumulation to 0.63/0.37); Red-Team adopted on the China measured-NBS floor (a real hit: the Auditor established the correct principle — the resolver uses NBS, so forecast the MEASURED series and do not shade for the widely-argued 0.5-2 pp overstatement — and then used 2.6% anyway, which is an internal inconsistency, not a judgement) and partially adopted on Baumol double-counting; Inside-View down-weighted on the care-rationing GDP leak's magnitude; Outside-View's reference class used only as the override it correctly identified.\n\nOECD-38 LEG = 0.95%/yr. Fermi's two routes agree: growth accounting gives labour input -0.20%/yr x 0.62 = -0.12; capital services +1.25%/yr expected (1.8 pre-bust, 1.0 post-bust, and 2032-2036 IS the post-bust half at the locked AI-Q4 66%) x 0.38 = +0.48; TFP 0.705 gross using the 2B regime-weighted AI increment 0.162(0.75) + 0.472(0.35) + 0.365(0.05) = +0.305 on a 0.40 baseline; frictions -0.30 (Baumol composition -0.15, informal-carer withdrawal -0.05, fiscal crowding of public investment -0.10) = 0.77; and the direct 2A route gives 0.6 per capita + 0.15 population + 0.305 - 0.30 = 0.76. RED-TEAM HIT PARTIALLY ACCEPTED on the Baumol term: public health and social-care output is deflated largely by input costs with no quality adjustment, and 2A's locked 0.6%/yr per-capita baseline is stated as the path \"absent productivity offsets,\" so some of the composition drag is already inside it; counting a full -0.15 again double-counts. I use -0.10, which with the upper end of the migration and capital-services terms gives 0.95. I do NOT follow him to 1.15, because his +0.45 gross automation pull is an assertion and the 2B regime weights do not support it.\n\nCHINA LEG = 3.05%/yr. Fermi: labour input -1.1%/yr x 0.52 = -0.57; capital services +4.0%/yr x 0.48 = +1.92 (property-driven deepening exhausted, ICOR rising); TFP +1.40; NBS measurement wedge +0.40 = 3.15, cross-checked at 3.0 against the locked -2.7 pp/yr drag read as back-loaded over 2025-2050 and therefore ~-2.2 pp realized by 2032-2036. Red-Team's 3.3 adds a political floor on top of the measurement wedge, which is partly double-counting the same thing. I take 3.05, above the Auditor's and Inside-View's 2.6-2.9 and below the Red-Team's 3.3.\n\nCOMBINED: 0.63(0.95) + 0.37(3.05) = 0.599 + 1.129 = 1.728 -> P50 1.75%/yr.\n\nTHE ARITHMETIC FINDING I CARRY FORWARD, FROM FERMI AND UNCHALLENGED BY ANYONE: the aging drag on THIS aggregate is larger than 2A's headline global figure, because the geography excludes India and Africa. China's 0.37 weight times its locked -2.7 pp = -1.00 pp on the combined aggregate by itself; the OECD leg's own ~-0.85 pp drag at a 0.63 weight adds -0.54 pp; total ~-1.54 pp, roughly 40% larger than the global -1.1 pp. Any panelist importing the global number understates the drag by ~0.44 pp, which is the difference between a 1.75% and a 2.2% P50. I also keep the Outside-View's honest override statement: no 5-year window in 65 years averaged below ~2.3% for this aggregate, so a P50 of 1.75 sits BELOW the entire historical support — justified only because the headcount is not forecastable away, and the size of that override is the single largest driver of every number in this payload.\n\nDISPERSION: sd ~0.72 pp on a 5-year annualized average (tails compress relative to annual growth; recession hazard ~0.45 inside the window at 0.5-0.7 pp on the average). P10 0.90 (bust plus care rationing plus the BIS Box C labour-income demand deficit plus China under 2%); P90 2.75 (no bust, Transformative-Breakout tail, China ~4%, migration liberalisation relieving the physical wall). Mildly LEFT-skewed, because the binding input — direct personal ADL care hours for the 80+ cohort, and grid interconnection plus firm dispatchable capacity — is a cap rather than a symmetric shock; I keep the Inside-View's weakest-link structure while rejecting his estimate of its GDP cost. Implied and carried verbatim into CQ6: P(>=2.6) = 0.12, P(<2.0) = 0.64, P(<1.7) = 0.47.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:52.823Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Turkish annual CPI, as published by TurkStat, averages below 7.0% over calendar 2027-2029 (arithmetic mean of the three December-on-December prints), with Mexico, Chile and Colombia each averaging below 3.5% over the same three years.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The cleanest single falsifier of MY numbers, because I have made this term the largest and most mechanical in the payload. It removes the ~+0.30 pp EM-OECD floor: my GDP-weighted OECD-38 core central falls from ~2.65 to ~2.45 and P(core >= 2.50 over 2027-2036) drops from 0.58 to ~0.38, taking CQ2 from 38 to ~26. The 2032-2036 central falls from 2.35 to ~2.18, cutting CQ6-C from 17 to ~11 and raising CQ6-D from 14 to ~20, with B roughly unchanged at ~57. CQ4 P50 falls ~15 bp.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US CPI core commodities (core goods) 12-month rate at or below 0.0% for calendar-year 2029, while Section 301/232-style tariff coverage remains in force at or above 2026 breadth.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the one Red-Team hit I partially adopted — downward nominal rigidity converting the relative-price skew into the level. My core-goods term reverts from 0.8% to the Wave-1 plug of ~0.4% (-0.09 pp on the US leg, ~-0.07 pp on the aggregate), and with it the whole claim that tariffs and fragmentation have changed the regime in sectors that 'want' to deflate. CQ2 falls from 38 to ~32; CQ6-C from 17 to ~13 with the mass to B; CQ5 conjunct A falls ~0.03, taking CQ5 from 27 to ~25. It would also mean the Red-Team contrarian-posed on his single largest departure from Wave 1 and I should not have moved at all.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI (NBS monthly series, any basket vintage) prints 2.0% year-on-year or higher for four consecutive quarters at any point between 2027 and 2031, with the non-food, non-residence component contributing at least 1.0 pp of it (i.e. not a pork or energy spike).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills CQ2's stronger conjunct and the China-as-semi-independent-deflationary-object premise my effort brief required me to carry. P(China CPI < 1.50% over 2027-2036) falls from 0.68 to ~0.38 and CQ2 drops from 38 to ~22 even with my OECD leg fully intact — which is the cleanest available test of whether my CQ2 comes from the OECD leg (my claim) or from the correlation assumption. It also lifts my China real leg from 3.05% toward 3.4%, raising CQ3 P50 from 1.75 to ~1.88 and shifting ~4 points of CQ6 mass out of D into B and C.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Cumulative publicly disclosed AI-related capex impairments and cancellations stay below 10% of announced hyperscaler buildouts AND net external AI software/API revenue exceeds 1.0x annualized AI compute D&A for four consecutive quarters, with at least 70 GW of net firm capacity serving data centres reaching commercial operation across OECD-38 + China (EIA / ENTSO-E / CEC commissioning data) by end-2031.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"The highest-leverage signpost for my payload, because it removes the locked AI-Q4 bust from the middle of every 2032-2036 measurement window and simultaneously refutes my coupled claim that trades labour and interconnection bind harder than the dollars. Capital-services growth stays at ~1.8%/yr (+0.30 pp on the OECD leg) and the AI savings-demand term returns to +0.25 pp on r*. CQ3 P50 moves from 1.75 to ~2.05 and P90 from 2.75 to ~3.00; CQ4 P50 from 2.00 to ~2.30 with a thinner left tail; CQ6-A from 12 to ~19, D from 14 to ~9, B to ~57, C ~15; CQ5 falls from 27 to ~21 as the back-half MOVE path drops toward 85 and the leveraged-unwind shock source thins. It would also retire my AI-Q3 coupling correction.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An equity drawdown of >= 20% (MSCI World, USD, closing basis) occurs in which the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yield FALLS by >= 100 bp between the equity peak date and the trough date — the classic flight-to-quality reasserting itself despite convenience-yield erosion, basis-trade absorption and >100% debt/GDP.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the fiscal-dominance correlation structure behind both CQ5 and CQ4's right tail. P(no 50 bp rally | drawdown) falls from 0.45 to ~0.28 and the A|B conditional from 0.65 to ~0.52, taking CQ5 from 27 to ~16. CQ4 P90 falls from 3.35 to ~2.95 and the distribution loses its right skew. ~3 points of CQ6 mass move from C to D, because bond-market tolerance means repression is unnecessary and accommodation of inflation is less likely.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 3 reconciler seat, effort 'inflation'."}}